docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:340: # "still a tombstone" and "already joined" at that instant depends on how many
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:341: # retirements the drain happened to complete while the workload was still
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:342: # running, and pinning either half would pin a race. What is timing-safe is the
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:343: # conservation law: every production row the reaps claimed is either still
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:344: # resident or already removed, so
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:345: #   resident + (removed - TOMBSTONE_FIXTURE_REMOVALS) == PRODUCTION_REAPED_ROWS
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:346: # is invariant across every scheduling of the same workload. It is asserted below
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:347: # on the LAST `[TOMBSTONE_CENSUS:` line, which is this sample: the other two
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:348: # census emitters on this arch (kernel_main's and the strand oracle's once-only
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:349: # report) both fire earlier, and the total emission count is pinned so a deleted
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:350: # sample cannot pass by leaving a stale earlier line in its place.
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:351: readonly TOMBSTONE_CENSUS_EMISSIONS=3
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:352: # (2) Quiesce, emitted from the idle loop once the deferred-reclaim queues are
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:353: # empty or a 2000 ms backstop elapses, whichever comes first. Before #653 it was
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:354: # always the backstop, and `pending` was nonzero because production reclamation
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:355: # was dead by then; the field was a bounded attribution field for exactly that
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:356: # reason. It is now an exact zero, and it is the load-bearing half of this pin:
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:357: # `pending=0:parked=0:resident=0` is the return-to-zero evidence x86 has never
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:358: # had. `removed` is the two fixture rows plus every production row
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:359: # PRODUCTION_REAPED_ROWS derives (see its own comment above), because
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:360: # a row can only be removed by the join after it has been both reaped and
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:361: # retired. The reap-second/retire-second SPLIT is deliberately not pinned: with
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:362: # the drain running during the userspace phase, a row may retire before or after
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:363: # its reap and the arm that completes the join differs accordingly. Their SUM is
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:364: # invariant and is asserted below.
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:365: #
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:366: # `pending` is NOT pinned to zero, and the reason is a measurement rather than a
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:367: # concession. A boot on the fixed kernel drains seventeen of the eighteen
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:368: # receipts #653 used to leak and leaves exactly one, because a page-table root
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:369: # cannot be retired while it is the root the CPU currently has installed — and
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:370: # after the last userspace thread exits, nothing on this uniprocessor profile
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:371: # ever loads another one. That receipt is not a leak the drain could have taken;
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:372: # refusing it is the root proof working. What must be pinned is that NOTHING
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:373: # RETIRABLE is left behind, which is what `pend_selectable=0` and the
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:374: # depth-conservation check below assert, and which the strand would have
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:375: # violated with seventeen selectable receipts.
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:376: readonly TOMBSTONE_JOINED_REMOVALS=$(( TOMBSTONE_FIXTURE_REMOVALS + PRODUCTION_REAPED_ROWS ))
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:377: TOMBSTONE_QUIESCE_PATTERN="\\[TOMBSTONE_QUIESCE:resident=0:removed=${TOMBSTONE_JOINED_REMOVALS}:reap_second=[0-9]+:retire_second=[0-9]+:abandoned_unqueued=1:pending=[0-9]+:parked=0\\]"
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:378: KSTACK_QUIESCE_LEAK_PATTERN='\[KSTACK_QUIESCE_LEAK:baseline_outstanding=[0-9]+:outstanding=[0-9]+:leaked=0\]'
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:379: # (3) #653 delta (3) — the drain's own refusal counters, which existed before the
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:380: # fix and were printed by nothing: a whole-boot loss of production reclamation
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:381: # was inferable only three phases later from a tombstone census read alongside a
docker/qemu/run-x86-boot-tests.sh:382: # queue depth. `context_violations=0` is exact (a drain entered under the process
